riscos-mesa 20.3.5-2 test programs (static ELF, GCCSDK GCC 10.2.0)
===========================================================

Needs: RISC OS 5, ARMv7 with VFP (Pi 2/3/4), SharedUnixLibrary and
ARMEABISupport loaded (same as the OpenTTD build). Best in a 16M-colour
screen mode. Run from a TaskWindow or F12 command line, in this directory.
The zip carries RISC OS filetypes: the programs unzip as ELF (&E1F) with
SparkFS or the RISC OS unzip.

Run in this order and note the output:

1. orient
   Checks row order and pixel byte order. Should print row0 = ff 00 00 ..,
   row15 = 00 00 ff ..  Exit code 0 means pass.

2. prof
   Lists which GL versions are granted. Expected: compat 2.1 works,
   3.x and core are NULL.

3. osmesatest 320 240 100
   Prints GL strings, fixed-function fps and GLSL fps, then saves the last
   frame as a Sprite file "gltest" in the current directory (open in Paint:
   a shaded cube on dark red). Try 640 480 50 too.

4. glutest
   GLU check. Prints GLU 1.3 and "centre px=ff".

5. sdlgltest 640 480
   SDL2 OpenGL with a spinning lit cube. Run from the desktop it opens in a
   normal desktop window; the title bar shows fps and each frame's render
   (Mesa) and present (plotting) time. Keys (click in the window first):
     F      toggle full screen and back
     Space  toggle vsync (in a window this yields to other tasks)
     Wheel  zoom in and out
     Escape quit (closing the window also works)
   Options: -f start full screen, -t N quit after N seconds,
   -r N cap at N fps using SDL_Delay, -w only redraw when something happens
   (idle = no CPU). It prints a summary (average fps, render/present ms)
   when it quits.

6. glbench [width height [seconds [scene]]] [-o file]
   Software GL benchmark, no desktop involved: clear, lit cube, full-screen
   texture, blending, 12288 triangles and GLSL, in ms per frame. Scene
   names: clear cube tex blend tris glsl. Use -o file to save the results
   (rather than "> file"). Reports the address and RISC OS error if
   anything crashes.

Please report: the fps figures, anything that crashes (with the error
text), the fps in the window title, whether the scroll wheel zooms (in a window
and in full screen), whether the cube redraws after you drag
another window over it, and whether F (full screen and back) leaves the
desktop redrawn properly.
